course: dictionary
lesson: 10993
title: Wartime and peacetime
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you about a time. Wartime is a period when a country is at war. «In wartime, children were sent away from cities to live in the countryside.» The word wartime tells you when this happened: during a war.
explain: Wartime can also describe things. In «wartime food», the noun food is the thing, and wartime tells you what kind of food it was: food from a time of war, when shops had less to sell.
explain: Peacetime is the opposite. It is a period when a country is not at war. Knowing an opposite helps you remember a word, so think of wartime and peacetime as a pair.
explain: Stories set «in wartime» often show brave people and families who are kept apart. Read the clues around the word to picture the time: an air-raid shelter, a ration book, a letter from far away.
fact: Wartime means a period when a country is at war.
fact: As an adjective, wartime describes things from that time, such as «wartime food».
fact: Peacetime is the opposite: a time when a country is not at war.
fact: In the Second World War, many British children were evacuated, which means sent away from cities to safer places in the countryside.
fact: The idea that carrots give you night vision came from «wartime propaganda», a made-up story spread on purpose during a war.
exercise: What does wartime mean? | A period when a country is having a big party | A period when shops close early | *A period when a country is at war | A period when it snows every day
exercise: In «wartime food», what does the word wartime tell you? | Where the food was grown | *What kind of food it was, from a time of war | How many people ate the food | The name of the cook
exercise: What is the opposite of wartime? | Teatime | *Peacetime | Bedtime | Playtime
test: «In wartime, children were sent to live in the countryside.» Why were they sent away? | To go on a holiday with their friends | To help grow vegetables for the shops | To learn to swim | *To be safe from bombing in the cities
test: In «wartime food», which word is the adjective? | both words | food | *wartime | neither word
exam: «The carrot story came from wartime propaganda, a made-up story spread on purpose during a war.» What does propaganda mean here? | A kind of night vision | A school lesson about carrots | *A made-up story spread on purpose during a war | A tasty vegetable dish
